@body

![Slide image 4](assets/lect06d-x_linked_genes_1/image_004.png)

@notes

Source: lect06d-x_linked_genes_1.odp, slide 7.

=== layout: one-panel
# Wild Type

@body

- Morgan noted [*wild type*]{color=red underline=true}, or normal, phenotypes that were common in the fly populations
  - [*wild type*]{color=red underline=true} literally means the most common phenotype
- Traits alternative to the [*wild type*]{color=red underline=true} are called [*mutant*]{color=blue underline=true} phenotypes

@notes

Source: lect06d-x_linked_genes_1.odp, slide 8.

=== layout: one-panel

@body

![Slide image 5](assets/lect06d-x_linked_genes_1/image_005.jpg)

- Wild-type Drosophila flies have red eyes (left).
- Among his flies, Morgan discovered a mutant male with white eyes (right).
- This variation made it possible for Morgan to trace a gene for eye color to a specific chromosome (LMs).

@notes

Source: lect06d-x_linked_genes_1.odp, slide 9.

=== layout: one-panel
# Correlating Behavior of a Gene’s Alleles with Behavior of a Chromosome Pair

@body

- In one experiment, Morgan mated male flies with white eyes (mutant) with female flies with red eyes (wild type)
  - The F1 generation all had red eyes
  - The F2 generation showed a 3\:1 red to white eye ratio, but [only males had white eyes]{color=blue}
- Morgan determined that the white-eyed mutant allele must be located on the X chromosome
- Morgan’s finding supported the chromosome theory of inheritance

@notes

Source: lect06d-x_linked_genes_1.odp, slide 10.

