# Z-linked inheritance in chickens

@left

![Slide image 15](assets/lect06d-x_linked_genes_1/image_015.jpg)

@right

- Females are heterogametic (WZ)
- Males are homogametic (ZZ)
- Barred feathers in chickens, a classic example showing that chromosomal sex determination in birds is the reverse of that in mammals.

@notes

Source: lect06d-x_linked_genes_1.odp, slide 23.

=== layout: two-panels

# Haplo-diploid system

@left

- In most bees and ants, females develop from fertilized eggs and are [*diploid*]{underline=true}.
- Males develop from unfertilized eggs and are [*haploid*]{underline=true}.
- This system does not use sex chromosomes.

@right

![Haplo-diploid system](assets/lect06d-x_linked_genes_1/haplodiploid_system.svg)

@notes

