=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

Duchenne muscular dystrophy is caused by an X-linked recessive allele. How likely is a woman to have this condition?

- A) Women can never have this condition.
- B) One-fourth of an affected man's daughters would have it.
- C) Half of an affected father and carrier mother's daughters could have it.
- D) Only women with three X chromosomes could have it.

@answer

Answer: C

@notes

Source: lect06d-x_linked_genes_1.odp, slide 51. | Original prompt: multiple-choice @question  Duchenne muscular dystrophy is a serious condition caused by a recessive allele of a gene on the human X chromosome. The patients have muscles that weaken over time because they have absent or decreased dystrophin, a muscle protein. They rarely live past their twenties. How likely is it for a woman to have this condition?  - A) Women can NEVER have this condition. - B) One-fourth (25%) of the daughters of an affected man would have this condition. - C) One-half (50%) of the daughters of an affected father and a carrier mother could have this condition. - D) Only if a woman is XXX (three X chromosomes) could she have this condition.  @answer  [Answer\: C]{color=red} 

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

A man with _achondroplasia and normal vision_ marries a _color-blind woman of normal height._ His father and both of her parents have normal height. Achondroplasia is autosomal dominant; color blindness is X-linked recessive. How many daughters would be color-blind and have achondroplasia?

- A) None
- B) One out of four
- C) Half
- D) Three out of four
- E) All

@answer

Answer: A

@notes

Source: lect06d-x_linked_genes_1.odp, slide 52.

